/test_test_status_in_progress.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py", "tests/test_verify_resolutions_lineage.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-coherence-pass.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/coherence-pass.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "archive-change-log's new remedy can dead-end: update-gitignore --dry-run never reports the archive directory while it does not exist yet", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke, in one sentence: the refusal (`change_log_archive.archive_would_be_ignored`) asks git about `.prawduct/change-log-archive/` unconditionally, but the command it now points at only checks the on-demand committed paths (`CommittedPath(..., always=False)`) that already exist on disk (`core._committed_in_scope`), and archive-change-log refuses BEFORE creating the archive. So in a repo whose `.gitignore` is `.prawduct/*` with `!` lines for every always-present path and `change-log.md`, but not the archive (hand-narrowed, or narrowed before the archive joined `V2_COMMITTED_PATHS`), `update-gitignore --dry-run` reports nothing ignored, prints no lines, and the re-run is refused the same way. Before this change the message spelled out the line that fixed exactly this case (`!.prawduct/change-log-archive/`). The new test (tests/test_change_log_archive.py) only follows the remedy under a wholesale `.prawduct/` rule, where always-present paths are hidden too, so the replacement block happens to include the archive line. Learnings rule: 'A REMEDY named in a checklist must be tested against the predicate that produced the condition'. Fix it with one owner both sides go through: have the refusal print `core.describe_ignored_by_rule` for the archive path it just tested (its own probe result), or have the dry run check a path a caller names even when it is absent. Then add the narrowed-without-archive `.gitignore` as a test case that follows the remedy to a successful `--apply`. The same gap applies to any future remedy about an on-demand path that may not exist yet (`releases/`, `runbooks/`, the operator-verification queue): search for refusals that point at `update-gitignore` about such a path.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/test_change_log_archive.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Moving the head-detach check into consolidate drops it from every refusal path, including the tree-changed refusal a reviewer's checkout causes", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke, in one sentence: `cmd_critic_consolidate` used to read the manifest's branch before consolidating and print the `head_guard` warning whatever `consolidate` returned, but `_lead_with_moved_head` now runs only on the incomplete no-op and in the success record's `next_action`. Every exit-1 path in `critic_consolidate.consolidate` (invalid partial, tree changed since dispatch, failed fact or resolution append, waiver failure) now says nothing about HEAD on the explicit path too. The tree-changed refusal is the case that matters: a reviewer that detaches HEAD at a DIFFERENT commit changes the working tree, so consolidation refuses with `_tree_changed_refusal`, which tells the builder to 'revert it, then critic-discard' and never says HEAD left the branch or gives `git switch <branch>`, the actual repair. Every new test in TestEveryConsolidationReportsAMovedHead uses `_detach_at_tip` (same tree), so none reaches a refusal path. Lead the refusal messages with the same sentence: read `manifest.get('branch')` once after `validate_manifest`, and route the fail-closed prints through the helper, or at least `_tree_changed_refusal`. Add a test that detaches at another commit and asserts the refusal names the branch and `git switch`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/test_native_review_head.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The findings-cache read is copied at every reader and the UTF-8 fix was another site-by-site sweep", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: no function owns reading `.prawduct/.critic-findings.json`, so each reader has its own copy of `json.loads(path.read_text(encoding=...))` and its own except tuple. This bundle fixed the decode one site at a time (critic_mode._fresh_cumulative_covers_head, critic_mode._verify_prior_record, briefing._summarize_critic_findings, gates.validate_critic_findings, ledger.ledger_append, critic_consolidate.verify_anchor). Other copies sit in critic_consolidate (around lines 2144, 6405 and 6779). The comment this bundle deleted from verify_anchor said this shape 're-seeded this class three times'. Per core.md, 'A review finding names an instance; the defect is a class ... fix the class through one owner.' Add one reader, e.g. `read_findings_cache(prawduct_dir) -> (dict | None, Exception | None)`, that decodes UTF-8 and catches (OSError, ValueError). Route every reader through it, verify_anchor included. To bound the set, grep `critic-findings.json` and `findings_path.read_text`. Without an owner, the next reader copies a site without the decode or the ValueError arm, and briefing or mode inference raises a traceback on an undecodable cache again.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/ledger.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Doctor checks are cited by name now, but only one name is tested against doctor's list", "recommendation": "Class. The bundle swaps one moving anchor (an ordinal) for another (a check name), and the guard only half-covers the new one. test_doctor_check_named_not_numbered.py fails on any `check #N`. But it checks only LEGACY_LOG_CHECK against doctor's `N. **Name**` list. The other names this bundle introduced have no such check: *Install reference*, *Static governance anchor*, *Notes transport configured* (onboard), *Gitignore contract* (doctor-vs-janitor, janitor themes), *API versioning decision*, *External backlog files*, and *Leftover norm-index scaffold rows* (norm_probes trigger_summary). All of them resolve today; I checked each. If someone renames a doctor check, an agent following the routing searches doctor's SKILL.md for a heading that no longer exists. The construction is to extend the test: extract every cited doctor check name from shipped prose and printed literals (the `*X* check` / `*X* procedure` / `\"X\" check` shapes next to `/prawduct:doctor`) and assert each one prefixes a `^\\d+\\. \\*\\*` item in plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md. Include a positive control, a fake name that must fail.", "files": ["tests/test_doctor_check_named_not_numbered.py", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "plugin/docs/doctor-vs-janitor.md", "plugin/skills/janitor/themes.md", "plugin/lib/norm_probes.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "critic-reviewer.md still says the builder ran the tests before review, a claim this bundle deleted from SKILL.md as false", "recommendation": "Instance. Chunk 01 cut 'the builder runs tests before requesting review' from plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md, and its budget entry in test_v5_methodology.py calls the sentence 'false since the suite moved to the boundary'. The same claim is still in plugin/agents/critic-reviewer.md lines 17-18 ('Review through code analysis only; the builder ran the tests before requesting review'). This bundle edited that file, but only its tools: line. A dispatched reviewer now gets told two things that conflict: `test-status` says `running:` or `stale:`, and its contract says the suite already ran. It may resolve that by trusting the contract and treating stale evidence as a recording lapse. Delete the clause and lower the dispatched-reviewer payload ceilings with it. Per core.md, 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees: search for the claim, not the tokens you edited.'", "files": ["plugin/agents/critic-reviewer.md"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "blocking", "title": "check-operator-verification's lib-import arm now fails closed, departing from api-contract's recorded Error Model decision that names it as a fail-open gate, with no recorded decision there", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the plan's governed_by was seeded against architecture and data-model only, so the artifact that actually rules this command's fail direction was never consulted. `.prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md` Error Model (the `api_error_model_approach` recorded decision) says, under 'Fail-direction is deliberate and per-purpose': 'Unevaluable *advisory* gate (an optional lib path failed to import) -> fail-open, exit 0: an ungradeable gate must never false-block (`classify-diff-risk`, `check-operator-verification`, `check-plugin-active`)', and scopes that rule to the plugin's own lib failing to import. `cmd_check_operator_verification` in plugin/bin/prawduct-hook now returns 3 on exactly that ImportError, and argues the opposite position in its comment. That is unmarked prose recording a decision, so it binds (Normative authority): syncing it to the code would silently unmake it. Consequence: a maintainer following the owning artifact restores fail-open to 'conform', or applies the fail-open rule to a new gate with check-operator-verification as the cited example. The other two gates on the list are still fail-open, and nothing explains the exception. The plan's disposition 'authority fails closed; advice fails soft -> conforms' records a reason against architecture, but it is not a vetoable decision against the rule it departs from. Fix: record the departure beneath api-contract's fail-direction clause as a `[DECISION: ... | user can veto/override]` (check-operator-verification blocks /prawduct:pr create, so it is authority, not advice) with the owner's confirmation, and leave the clause itself alone per 'Editing a norm's STATEMENT in the same commit as the code it would bless is the amend-to-match-own-code tell'. Or revert the arm to exit 0. Add api-contract to the plan's governed_by, and run `prawduct-hook jurisdiction` to check that no other governing artifact was missed.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint governed-by-gap: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-coherence-pass.md:10", "recommendation": "architecture carries 8 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 3 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-coherence-pass.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint governed-by-gap: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-coherence-pass.md:15", "recommendation": "data-model carries 8 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 2 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-coherence-pass.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-8"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 7, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "transfer_remedy still writes its own run-the-suite clause beside suite_remedy", "recommendation": "The new `suite_remedy` docstring says every surface prints the owner's clause. `gates.transfer_remedy` still writes its own 'Run the suite and `prawduct-hook test-evidence record`' for the not-current and interrupted states and in the uncommitted-paths branch (that wording drops `RUN_AND_RECORD`'s `--from-junit` route). It uses the owner's clause only for failing and degraded. The texts agree today and nobody is misdirected, so no edit is owed. If the one-owner claim is meant literally, `suite_step.remedy` (or `RUN_AND_RECORD`) is the clause to use in those branches.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: the recorded dispositions are true of the diff", "recommendation": "Checked all five recorded `governed_by:` dispositions against the diff. Architecture 'authority fails closed; advice fails soft': the ImportError arm of check-operator-verification now exits 3, and an off gate's unreadable-queue note carries `_OFF_TAIL` with no blocking voice. 'Every fact has one home': the suite remedy, the narrowing advice and the head-detach check each lose a second owner; the transfer_remedy residue is the observation above. 'Guides and reviews, never implements': holds. Data-model 'facts immutable/append-only': no fact is rewritten; the manifest drops `chunk` and `native_review.text_path`, and the ledger's one reader moved to `plan_status.fact_chunks`. 'Derived views never authoritative': `verify_anchor` now defers a missing or undecodable cache to `select_branch_review` over the store. No departure found. The undisposed `## Direction` norms are record-lint's `governed-by-gap` and are not raised here.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "briefing calls critic_consolidate's private _recorded_branch, behind a broad except", "recommendation": "briefing._this_branchs_record calls `critic_consolidate._recorded_branch(foreign)`. If someone renames that private helper, the AttributeError lands in the display-only broad except. The briefing then quietly falls back to the 'Could not confirm' lead and shows another branch's counts, which is the behaviour chunk 03 removed. Either have verify_anchor return the foreign review's branch next to `foreign`, or make the helper public. The tests probably catch a rename today, so this is an observation.", "files": ["plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "verify_anchor's answer is an untagged union of five fields that its consumers branch on differently", "recommendation": "verify_anchor returns {fact, reason, rederived, branch_commits, foreign}, and which fields are meaningful depends on the others. foreign is only set when fact is None, and rederived only when fact is set. briefing tells the cached review from a re-derived one by comparing `fact['id']` with the cache's fact_id. critic_mode and _prior_review_fact read `rederived`. The two readings agree today because `none`/`as_cached` build the dict. A `status` discriminator ('cached' | 'rederived' | 'none' | 'none-foreign') would give every consumer the same switch. Borderline: the result is local and not persisted.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "test-status builds the in-flight finding and its remedy from two separate reads of the record", "recommendation": "The `running:` line used to come from one `_in_progress_parts` tuple: state, finding and remedy together. Now the finding comes from `_in_progress_now` and the remedy from `suite_remedy(project_dir)`, which reads the record again. If a recorder finishes between the two reads, the line pairs 'running' with 'run the suite and record', the second-suite advice #1178 exists to prevent. When progress is not None, take the remedy from the same parts tuple. The race window is small.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The gate-off note re-renders QueueUnreadable from exc.__cause__", "recommendation": "_load_queue builds QueueUnreadable's message in blocking voice when it raises. The gate-off path then throws that message away and rebuilds one from `exc.__cause__ or exc`. With no __cause__ the rebuilt text would name `QueueUnreadable` as the error type. Store the underlying OSError/UnicodeDecodeError on the exception, render it at each caller with the voice its `blocks` needs, and the raise site no longer has to choose a voice.", "files": ["plugin/lib/operator_verification.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Empty KNOWN allowlist in the doctor-ordinal guard", "recommendation": "`KNOWN: dict[str, int] = {}` and its comment ('outside this change's files, reported for their owners to fix') are scaffolding nothing uses, and the comment narrates this change. Assert `hits == {}`, as the prose test already does, and delete KNOWN.", "files": ["tests/test_doctor_check_named_not_numbered.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The one owner of the narrowing advice covers only in-repo .gitignore rules", "recommendation": "The `fragment-present-untracked` remedy (coverage._fragment_check_without_history and PR Step 1c) and archive-change-log now point at `update-gitignore --dry-run`. That command reports only rules from the repo's shared .gitignore files (`_shared_ignore_matches`). When `.prawduct/changes/` is ignored from `.git/info/exclude` or a global excludes file, the dry run prints nothing relevant. Before this bundle the advice spelled out lines that worked whatever the source. The configuration is rare. A clause in the remedy for that case ('or the rule is outside this repo's .gitignore') would close it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Doctor-check ordinals survive outside the guard's corpus", "recommendation": "The new guard (tests/test_doctor_check_named_not_numbered.py) covers plugin/ only, which matches the change fragment's 'every shipped prose file'. Ordinals remain in repo docs and records: documentation/release-process.md:349 ('whose check #22 runs migrate-change-log'; the printed line it narrates now names the check), documentation/MIGRATION.md:123 ('Health Check #4'), and many 'Health Check #N' sites in .prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md and nonfunctional-requirements.md. Every one still resolves to the right check today, so nothing misleads yet. They go stale silently the next time doctor renumbers. Widening the guard's roots to documentation/ would close the class, and no edit is owed by this change.", "files": ["tests/test_doctor_check_named_not_numbered.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Guard's KNOWN comment narrates the change that wrote it", "recommendation": "`KNOWN`'s comment reads 'User-facing ordinals outside this change's files, reported for their owners to fix'. The dict is empty, and 'this change' has no referent once the change merges. Delete the clause, or say what an entry means (a tolerated per-file count).", "files": ["tests/test_doctor_check_named_not_numbered.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: oracle set covered, no bar cleared", "recommendation": "Covered: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-coherence-pass.md, .prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md, .prawduct/changes/coherence-pass.md. Checked against code: the data-model `chunk` sentence (the singular field stopped being written in 6c18b0f3, after v3.8.1, and plugin.json is 3.8.2-dev.1, so 'v3.8.1 and earlier' holds); the ledger reads `fact_chunks(manifest)[0]`; the manifest no longer writes `chunk` or `native_review.text_path`; head-detach goes through `consolidate` on all three callers (explicit, subagent-stop, Stop backstop); check-operator-verification's off-gate note and exit 3; contract-ignored-by-rule is `warn` with changed evidence, so its id changes as the consumer note says; archive-change-log and the PR gate point at `update-gitignore --dry-run`, which prints `describe_ignored_by_rule`. No claim ships false or misleads into action. The governed-by-gap counts are record-lint's and are not restated here.", "oid": "O-11"}], "duration_seconds": 300, "scope": "coherence-pass", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "sc